    "id": "art_modes-dorian-neck",
    "slug": "modes-dorian-neck",
    "title": "Raise the Sixth With the Drone Still Going",
    "summary": "Raising the b6 to a 6 is one fret under your own fingers, in a window you've owned since minor-caged — and in the G form all three of its b6s move without a single dot leaving the frame.",
    "tags": ["modes", "fretboard", "ear"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "On a keyboard, raising the sixth is an instruction about note names — swap one letter for another and move on. Here it's frets moving under real fingers, with a root still sounding underneath you, and the sound changes while your hand is doing it. That's the whole reason this lesson exists."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The frame doesn't move. Same five windows, same places, anchored on the root — "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Window Stays, the Third Moves",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-window-stays-put"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already made that case, and it holds here too. There's no Dorian shape to learn on top of it — only degrees to move inside a window you already own."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Start in the G form, frets 1 to 5. Here's "
        },
        {
          "text": "A Natural minor",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", then "
        },
        {
          "text": "A Dorian",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — drawn as everything in the window, not a grip a hand would hold. Eighteen dots both times. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Darkest Window",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-scale-g-form"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " called this window the one that holds three "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s, three "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s and three "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s at once — more of natural minor's flattened degrees than any other window carries. Dorian removes exactly the note that made it dark."
        }
      ]
    },
